    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Command and Control — Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols", "technique": "T1071.001", "what_happened": "At 2027-09-14 02:11:07 CEST, Suricata logged the first of a series of outbound HTTP POST requests from data.srcip=10.66.20.84 to data.dstip=198.18.0.51 on data.dstport=80. Each POST carried Content-Type application/octet-stream with a small high-entropy base64 body and targeted random-looking benign-themed URIs (/admin/_static/v3/blob.js, /static/v1/styles.css, /api/internal/heartbeat, /_/cdn/edge). The requests recurred at an almost-fixed ~60-second interval with small jitter — the canonical Sliver C2 HTTP profile beacon. Suricata fired 'ET MALWARE Sliver C2 Framework HTTP Check-in' (signature_id 2034969) and the custom Wazuh rule 92545 'Periodic HTTP POST application/octet-stream beacon with jitter'.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET MALWARE Sliver C2 Framework HTTP Check-in' (sig_id 2034969) with data.srcip=10.66.20.84 → data.dstip=198.18.0.51, data.dstport=80, repeated at regular ~60s intervals. The periodicity + application/octet-stream POST body + Sliver-profile URI set is the implant check-in fingerprint, not user-driven web traffic.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T02:11:07+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Command and Control — Application Layer Protocol: DNS", "technique": "T1071.004", "what_happened": "At 02:16:30 CEST, after the HTTP beacon series, the same host data.srcip=10.66.20.84 began emitting DNS queries for pseudo-random high-entropy subdomains under the parent domain cdn-update.zip (e.g. aG9zdC1jaGVjay1pbg.cdn-update.zip). These are Sliver's DNS-tunnel fallback channel — the implant rotates to DNS when HTTP is unavailable or for redundancy. Suricata fired the custom Wazuh rule 92546 'DNS tunnel — high-entropy subdomains' and 'ET MALWARE Possible DNS Tunneling'.", "chain_reason": "Repeated DNS queries from data.srcip=10.66.20.84 for distinct high-entropy labels under one parent zone (cdn-update.zip) at ~30s intervals. The pseudo-random subdomain pattern + the .zip TLD newly-registered parent + the regular cadence is DNS-tunnel C2, the documented Sliver fallback transport.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T02:16:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Command and Control — Encrypted Channel: Mutual TLS", "technique": "T1071.001", "what_happened": "At 02:20:55 CEST the host data.srcip=10.66.20.84 opened a TLS connection to data.dstip=198.18.0.51 that Suricata flagged as a Sliver mutual-TLS handshake — client certificate presented, self-signed server certificate, JA3 hash matching the known Sliver implant TLS fingerprint. Suricata fired 'ET MALWARE Sliver C2 Framework TLS'. This is Sliver's primary high-confidentiality transport; the implant escalated from HTTP/DNS staging to mTLS once a stable channel was confirmed.", "chain_reason": "Suricata TLS alert with data.srcip=10.66.20.84 → data.dstip=198.18.0.51, self-signed server certificate + client-cert mutual TLS + JA3 fingerprint matching the published Sliver implant signature. mTLS to the same C2 IP already seen in the HTTP beacon ties all three transports to one implant.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T02:20:55+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Resource Development — Obtain Capabilities: Tool", "technique": "T1588.002", "what_happened": "Across the 02:11-02:23 window Wazuh correlated the three transports (HTTP beacon, DNS tunnel, mTLS) to a single endpoint implant. The Sysmon telemetry on agent WIN-FIN-08 (10.66.20.84) shows an unsigned binary in a non-standard path making the outbound connections. The combination — Sliver-profile HTTP URIs, Sliver DNS-tunnel labels, Sliver JA3 — identifies the adversary tooling as the open-source Sliver C2 framework obtained from GitHub. MITRE T1588.002 (Obtain Capabilities: Tool) was attached because the offensive capability is a publicly-available adversary framework now operating inside the estate.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh correlation rule 'Multi-transport C2 beaconing from single internal host' fired on >=3 distinct C2 signatures (HTTP/DNS/TLS) sharing data.srcip=10.66.20.84 within a 15-minute window. The Sliver framework fingerprint across all three transports drove the T1588.002 mapping.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T02:23:00+0200"}
